# The client

The client is the website's update and its boot. Most messages change one field and run nothing. Two do more: the example advances a frame at a time while it plays, and the picker listens across the whole document while it is armed. Every other animation on the site is an SVG that drives itself.

## The update return

The update returns the next model and the commands to run — the pair every Foldkit update returns.

=>

import { Array, Effect, Match, Option, pipe, Schema as S, Stream } from 'effect'
import { Runtime, Subscription } from 'foldkit'
import { define, mapMessages, type Command } from 'foldkit/command'
import { ssrHydration } from '@athrio/foldkit-hydration'
import { view } from './view'
import {
  AimedAt,
  AimedAway,
  CancelledPick,
  CopyReset,
  GrewCard,
  type Message,
  Model,
  type Note,
  PickedTarget,
  PickingApplied,
  type Player,
  SectionScrolled,
  SelectedSection,
  SpottedSection,
  StageMoved,
  Ticked,
  ToggledFullHeight,
} from './model'
import { aimableAt, highlightOf, pathOf, routeOf, seedNotes, targetOf } from './devtools'
import { CHARACTERS, RUN, struckBy } from './example'

type Step = readonly [Model, ReadonlyArray<Command<Message>>]

## The timed commands

One command carries a timer. Copying writes the text to the clipboard, waits out the flash, and resets — the write may fail on a browser that refuses the clipboard, so it flashes regardless of whether it landed, since a button that silently does nothing is worse than one that lies.

=>

const CopyThenReset = define('CopyThenReset', { text: S.String }, CopyReset)(
  ({ text }) =>
    Effect.tryPromise(() => navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)).pipe(
      Effect.ignore,
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sleep('1400 millis')),
      Effect.as(CopyReset()),
    ),
)


## Jumping to a section

An outline row points at a heading by id, and clicking the row scrolls that heading into view. The scroll waits a beat first, so the browser measures where the heading sits after the row has redrawn rather than before. `scrollIntoView` scrolls the window itself, and the heading clears the fixed bar by its own `scroll-margin-top` from the stylesheet. A heading that never rendered — a chapter the reader has not opened — does nothing.

=>

const scrollIntoPreview = (id: string): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById(id)), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (target) => target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }),
  })

const ScrollToSection = define('ScrollToSection', { id: S.String }, SectionScrolled)(
  ({ id }) =>
    Effect.sleep('30 millis').pipe(
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => scrollIntoPreview(id))),
      Effect.as(SectionScrolled()),
    ),
)

## Moving the stage under the reader

The transport moves the stage to three places, and `StageMark` names them. The caret is where the run wants it while the document is being typed. The foot is where skipping and the end of a run leave it. The head is where the back button puts it, since a reader asking for the document from the start is asking to see its first line, not the line they happened to stop on.

An editor does not creep a line at a time. It holds still while the caret runs down the visible text, then steps when the caret would fall off the bottom. So the caret's mark scrolls three lines on rather than one, and only once the caret is within three lines of the foot. A line is twenty-eight pixels, which is the stage's own size at its own leading.

Every mark is clamped to the room the stage actually has, which is what answers a stage with nothing to scroll: the room is zero or less, and every mark comes out at the top.

=>

const LINE = 28
const TAIL = LINE * 3

const StageMark = S.Literals(['caret', 'foot', 'head'])
type StageMark = typeof StageMark.Type

const markedTop = (mark: StageMark, stage: HTMLElement): number =>
  Match.value(mark).pipe(
    Match.when('head', () => 0),
    Match.when('foot', () => stage.scrollHeight - stage.clientHeight),
    Match.when('caret', () => stage.scrollTop + TAIL),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const moveStage = (mark: StageMark): void =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById('loom-stage')), {
    onNone: () => undefined,
    onSome: (stage) => {
      const room = stage.scrollHeight - stage.clientHeight
      stage.scrollTop = Math.max(0, Math.min(room, markedTop(mark, stage)))
    },
  })

~

The foot waits a beat before it measures. It arrives with the whole rest of the document — and, at the end of a run, the two command buttons under it — so the stage it has to reach is taller than the one the browser has drawn. The other two marks measure at once, because neither depends on how tall the document has become.

=>

const MoveStage = define('MoveStage', { to: StageMark }, StageMoved)(
  ({ to }) =>
    (to === 'foot' ? Effect.sleep('30 millis') : Effect.void).pipe(
      Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => moveStage(to))),
      Effect.as(StageMoved()),
    ),
)

## Gliding to the card before it opens

Growing the card is worth nothing if the reader cannot see the whole of it, so the page comes to the card before the card takes the height. The two do not happen together: the sheet's top comes to rest first, and only then does the height open under it. Running them at once reads as the page and the card both lurching, and the reader loses which of the two moved.

The card keeps its place in the page and opens downwards, so its top does not move while the page glides. `CARD_AIR` is the air left above it when the page arrives. A page already standing within four pixels of that skips the glide, because a scroll that short reads as a twitch.

=>

const CARD_AIR = 24
const GLIDE = 320
const NUDGE = 4

const cardRest = (): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById('loom-card')),
    (card) => card.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY - CARD_AIR,
  )

~

The page is tweened by hand rather than left to `scrollTo`'s own smooth behaviour, because the glide has to say when it has finished and the browser's does not. Each frame reads the real clock, so a late frame lands where the elapsed time says it should rather than where the frame count would have put it, and the glide always takes its three hundred and twenty milliseconds.

The easing runs slow, fast, slow — a cubic either side of the halfway point. A page that starts and stops abruptly reads as a jump; this reads as the page being carried.

=>

const eased = (part: number): number =>
  part < 0.5 ? 4 * part * part * part : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * part + 2, 3) / 2

const glide = (from: number, distance: number, started: number): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.sync(() => Math.min(1, (performance.now() - started) / GLIDE)).pipe(
    Effect.tap((part) =>
      Effect.sync(() => window.scrollTo(0, Math.round(from + distance * eased(part)))),
    ),
    Effect.flatMap((part) =>
      part < 1
        ? Effect.sleep('16 millis').pipe(Effect.andThen(glide(from, distance, started)))
        : Effect.void,
    ),
  )

const GlideToCard = define('GlideToCard', GrewCard)(
  Effect.suspend(() =>
    Option.match(cardRest(), {
      onNone: () => Effect.void,
      onSome: (rest) => {
        const from = window.scrollY
        return Math.abs(rest - from) <= NUDGE
          ? Effect.void
          : glide(from, rest - from, performance.now())
      },
    }),
  ).pipe(Effect.as(GrewCard())),
)

## Arming the picker

An armed picker changes the cursor over the whole page, not just over one section, so the change belongs on the document's root element beside the theme and the accent. `ApplyPicking` sets a `data-picking` attribute while the picker is armed and removes it when it is not, and the stylesheet turns that into a crosshair. Keeping the rule in the stylesheet means the look stays where every other look on this site is written.

=>

const ApplyPicking = define('ApplyPicking', { picking: S.Boolean }, PickingApplied)(
  ({ picking }) =>
    Effect.sync(() =>
      picking
        ? document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-picking', 'on')
        : document.documentElement.removeAttribute('data-picking'),
    ).pipe(Effect.as(PickingApplied())),
)

## Filing a note

Sending is the one devtools message that does more than set a field. An empty draft sends nothing, so a stray press of enter costs nothing. Otherwise the note takes the next number, the kind and pointer of whatever the reviewer aimed at, and the path of the page they are on; a note sent with nothing aimed at is a `chat` note about the page itself. Filing it clears the draft and the target together and swings the panel back to the open list, where the new note is.

=>

const filed = (model: Model): Step => {
  const text = model.draft.trim()
  if (text === '') {
    return [model, []]
  }
  const seq = model.seq + 1
  const { kind, pointer } = Option.match(model.aimed, {
    onNone: () => ({ kind: 'chat' as const, pointer: '' }),
    onSome: (target) => ({ kind: target.kind, pointer: target.pointer }),
  })
  const note: Note = { seq, kind, pointer, route: pathOf(model.route), text, addressed: false }
  return [
    {
      ...model,
      seq,
      notes: [...model.notes, note],
      draft: '',
      aimed: Option.none(),
      noteTab: 'open',
    },
    [],
  ]
}

## The update

Most messages set one field: the query, the focus clamped to the results, the loom tab's view. Following a link records the page it led to and closes the palette behind it. Opening a documentation chapter records its slug, clears the heading the outline was following — the previous chapter's headings are no longer on the page — and shuts the drawer behind the reader, since they have arrived where they were going. The bar's own message only writes when the answer changes, so a scroll that keeps it hidden costs no render. Opening the palette puts the focus on its first result; closing it clears the query, the focus and the caret together, so the palette opens empty the next time. Moving the caret records where it now sits, and the search settles a moment after the last keystroke. The example's messages move a position rather than set a flag: playing from the end starts again from the first character, a frame advances the clock and asks the stage to follow the caret, and skipping jumps both the clock and the stage to the foot. Going back to the start puts the clock, the height and the stage all back where they began — the document at its first character, the card down from full height, and the stage at the head of the document rather than wherever the reader stopped reading.

Asking for full height is the one press that changes nothing at once. It runs the glide and waits: the page arrives at the card, `GrewCard` comes back, and only then does the height open. Giving the height back is immediate, since nothing has to travel first. Asking for a command's file toggles it, so pressing the same button twice closes what it opened. Selecting a section sets the active one and asks the preview to scroll to it. A section spotted while the reader scrolls sets the active one too, but runs nothing, so the outline follows the scroll rather than fighting it. Copying flashes the pressed button and runs its reset.

The devtools messages mostly set a field as well, and the interesting ones are those that turn a tool off on the way past. Arming the picker shuts the notes panel, since a reviewer taking aim wants the page in front of them; disarming it leaves the panel as it found it. Escape cancels the aim. Picking a target disarms the picker, records what was aimed at, and opens the panel over the composer, which is where the reviewer is going next. Collapsing the bar puts away the panel and the picker with it, because a bar folded to a handle should leave nothing of itself on the page. Every message that changes whether the picker is armed also asks the command to write that to the document, so the cursor and the model never disagree.

=>

const clamp = (value: number, max: number): number =>
  Math.max(0, Math.min(max, value))

const CHARS_PER_SECOND = 26

const toggled = (player: Player): Player => {
  if (player.playing) {
    return { ...player, playing: false }
  }
  const from = player.struck >= CHARACTERS ? 0 : player.struck
  return {
    ...player,
    struck: from,
    beat: from === 0 ? 0 : player.beat,
    playing: true,
    output: Option.none(),
  }
}

const stepped = (model: Model, delta: number): Step => {
  const beat = model.player.beat + (Math.min(delta, 32) / 1000) * CHARS_PER_SECOND
  if (beat >= RUN) {
    return [
      { ...model, player: { ...model.player, struck: CHARACTERS, beat: RUN, playing: false } },
      [MoveStage({ to: 'foot' })],
    ]
  }
  const struck = struckBy(beat)
  return [
    { ...model, player: { ...model.player, struck, beat } },
    struck === model.player.struck ? [] : [MoveStage({ to: 'caret' })],
  ]
}

const update = (model: Model, message: Message): Step =>
  Match.value(message).pipe(
    Match.withReturnType<Step>(),
    Match.tagsExhaustive({
      ToggledPlay: () => [{ ...model, player: toggled(model.player) }, []],
      Ticked: ({ delta }) => stepped(model, delta),
      SkippedToEnd: () => [
        { ...model, player: { ...model.player, struck: CHARACTERS, beat: RUN, playing: false } },
        [MoveStage({ to: 'foot' })],
      ],
      ReplayedDocument: () => [
        {
          ...model,
          player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
        },
        [MoveStage({ to: 'head' })],
      ],
      ToggledFullHeight: () =>
        model.player.full
          ? [{ ...model, player: { ...model.player, full: false } }, []]
          : [model, [GlideToCard()]],
      GrewCard: () => [{ ...model, player: { ...model.player, full: true } }, []],
      ShowedOutput: ({ which }) => [
        {
          ...model,
          player: {
            ...model.player,
            output: Option.contains(model.player.output, which)
              ? Option.none()
              : Option.some(which),
          },
        },
        [],
      ],
      StageMoved: () => [model, []],
      SelectedSection: ({ id }) => [{ ...model, activeSection: id }, [ScrollToSection({ id })]],
      SectionScrolled: () => [model, []],
      SpottedSection: ({ id }) =>
        id === model.activeSection ? [model, []] : [{ ...model, activeSection: id }, []],
      WentTo: ({ route }) => [{ ...model, route, searchOpen: false }, []],
      OpenedChapter: ({ slug }) => [
        { ...model, chapter: slug, activeSection: '', drawerOpen: false },
        [],
      ],
      PickedRuntime: ({ runtime }) => [{ ...model, runtime }, []],
      ScrolledBar: ({ hidden }) =>
        hidden === model.navHidden ? [model, []] : [{ ...model, navHidden: hidden }, []],
      OpenedDrawer: () => [{ ...model, drawerOpen: true }, []],
      ClosedDrawer: () => [{ ...model, drawerOpen: false }, []],
      OpenedSearch: () => [{ ...model, searchOpen: true, focus: 0 }, []],
      ClosedSearch: () => [
        { ...model, searchOpen: false, query: '', focus: 0, caret: 0, searching: false },
        [],
      ],
      MovedCaret: ({ at }) => [{ ...model, caret: at }, []],
      SettledSearch: () => [{ ...model, searching: false }, []],
      Typed: ({ query }) => [{ ...model, query, focus: 0 }, []],
      MovedFocus: ({ delta, count }) => [
        { ...model, focus: clamp(model.focus + delta, Math.max(0, count - 1)) },
        [],
      ],
      Copied: ({ id, text }) => [{ ...model, copied: id }, [CopyThenReset({ text })]],
      CopyReset: () => [{ ...model, copied: '' }, []],
      ToggledPicker: () => {
        const picking = !model.picking
        return [
          { ...model, picking, highlight: Option.none(), notesOpen: picking ? false : model.notesOpen },
          [ApplyPicking({ picking })],
        ]
      },
      PickingApplied: () => [model, []],
      CancelledPick: () => [
        { ...model, picking: false, highlight: Option.none() },
        [ApplyPicking({ picking: false })],
      ],
      AimedAt: ({ highlight }) => [{ ...model, highlight: Option.some(highlight) }, []],
      AimedAway: () => [{ ...model, highlight: Option.none() }, []],
      PickedTarget: ({ target }) => [
        {
          ...model,
          picking: false,
          highlight: Option.none(),
          aimed: Option.some(target),
          notesOpen: true,
          barCollapsed: false,
          noteTab: 'open',
        },
        [ApplyPicking({ picking: false })],
      ],
      ToggledNotes: () => [
        { ...model, notesOpen: !model.notesOpen, barCollapsed: false },
        [],
      ],
      ClosedNotes: () => [{ ...model, notesOpen: false }, []],
      CollapsedBar: () => [
        { ...model, barCollapsed: true, notesOpen: false, picking: false, highlight: Option.none() },
        [ApplyPicking({ picking: false })],
      ],
      ExpandedBar: () => [{ ...model, barCollapsed: false }, []],
      ShowedNotes: ({ tab }) => [{ ...model, noteTab: tab }, []],
      ResolvedNote: ({ seq }) => [
        {
          ...model,
          notes: Array.map(model.notes, (note) =>
            note.seq === seq ? { ...note, addressed: !note.addressed } : note,
          ),
        },
        [],
      ],
      DiscardedNote: ({ seq }) => [
        { ...model, notes: Array.filter(model.notes, (note) => note.seq !== seq) },
        [],
      ],
      ClearedTarget: () => [{ ...model, aimed: Option.none() }, []],
      DraftedNote: ({ text }) => [{ ...model, draft: text }, []],
      SentNote: () => filed(model),
    }),
  )

## Spotting the section on scroll

The outline should follow the reader, not only lead them. While the documentation is on show, a subscription watches the window scroll, finds the heading nearest the top of the article, and lifts that row in the outline. `activeSectionId` reads the article's own second-level headings — the same ones the outline lists — keeps the ones scrolled up past a small offset, and takes the last; when none has reached the offset yet, it falls back to the first.

=>

const SPY_OFFSET = 100
const SPY_TARGETS = '.docs-article h2[id]'

const activeSectionId = (): Option.Option<string> => {
  const targets = Array.fromIterable(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(SPY_TARGETS))
  return pipe(
    Array.last(Array.filter(targets, (el) => el.getBoundingClientRect().top <= SPY_OFFSET)),
    Option.orElse(() => Array.head(targets)),
    Option.map((el) => el.id),
    Option.filter((id) => id.length > 0),
  )
}

## Listening only when there is something to hear

Every listener here is conditional. Each one asks the model whether its work is happening at all, and a page with nothing playing, nothing armed and no outline on screen listens to nothing.

The picker needs the whole document, not one section, so it listens on the document rather than on any element the section renders. Three listeners run only while it is armed, and a fourth runs only while an outline is on screen. Two more have nothing to do with the picker: the frame loop that advances the clock while the document plays, and the escape key that gives the page back while the card stands at full height.

Both the pointer and the click listen in the capture phase, before the page's own handlers see the event. That is what lets a click on a link file a note instead of following the link: the mapper runs inside the browser's dispatch, so stopping the event there stops it in time.

A pointer over something the picker may aim at measures it and paints the outline. Over anything it may not — the bar, the panel, the page itself — the outline goes away rather than sticking to the last thing it touched. Escape disarms. And any scroll drops the outline, because a rectangle measured against the window is wrong the moment the page moves under it; the next pointer move measures again.

=>

const aimedMessage = (event: Event): Option.Option<Message> =>
  Option.some(
    pipe(
      aimableAt(event.target),
      Option.match({
        onNone: (): Message => AimedAway(),
        onSome: (element) => AimedAt({ highlight: highlightOf(element) }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const pickedMessage = (event: MouseEvent): Option.Option<Message> =>
  pipe(
    aimableAt(event.target),
    Option.map((element) => {
      event.preventDefault()
      event.stopPropagation()
      return PickedTarget({ target: targetOf(element) })
    }),
  )

const subscriptions = Subscription.make<Model, Message>()((entry) => ({
  sectionSpy: entry(
    { spying: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({
        spying: model.route === 'docs',
      }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ spying }) =>
        spying
          ? Subscription.fromEventFilterMap<Event, Message>({
              target: window,
              type: 'scroll',
              options: { passive: true },
              toMessage: () => Option.map(activeSectionId(), (id) => SpottedSection({ id })),
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  aiming: entry(
    { picking: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ picking: model.picking }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ picking }) =>
        picking
          ? Subscription.fromEventFilterMap<MouseEvent, Message>({
              target: document,
              type: 'mouseover',
              options: { capture: true },
              toMessage: aimedMessage,
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  picking: entry(
    { picking: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ picking: model.picking }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ picking }) =>
        picking
          ? Subscription.fromEventFilterMap<MouseEvent, Message>({
              target: document,
              type: 'click',
              options: { capture: true },
              toMessage: pickedMessage,
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  cancelling: entry(
    { picking: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ picking: model.picking }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ picking }) =>
        picking
          ? Subscription.fromEventFilterMap<KeyboardEvent, Message>({
              target: window,
              type: 'keydown',
              toMessage: (event) =>
                event.key === 'Escape' ? Option.some(CancelledPick()) : Option.none(),
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  typing: Subscription.animationFrame<Model, Message>({
    isActive: (model) => model.player.playing,
    toMessage: (delta) => Ticked({ delta }),
  }),
  shrinking: entry(
    { full: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ full: model.player.full }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ full }) =>
        full
          ? Subscription.fromEventFilterMap<KeyboardEvent, Message>({
              target: window,
              type: 'keydown',
              toMessage: (event) =>
                event.key === 'Escape' ? Option.some(ToggledFullHeight()) : Option.none(),
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
  unaiming: entry(
    { outlined: S.Boolean },
    {
      modelToDependencies: (model) => ({ outlined: Option.isSome(model.highlight) }),
      dependenciesToStream: ({ outlined }) =>
        outlined
          ? Subscription.fromEvent<Event, Message>({
              target: window,
              type: 'scroll',
              options: { passive: true, capture: true },
              toMessage: () => AimedAway(),
            })
          : Stream.empty,
    },
  ),
}))

## Booting

The client boots from a model. On a real load the server inlines the model it rendered the page with; in development Vite serves the shell without rendering anything, so the client falls back to a default. That default names the workspace version, since no server has fetched the latest release, and stands the example at its first frame. Importing the stylesheet here, in the browser entry, keeps it out of the server's path.

{{ version = ${version} }}

=>

import './landing.css'

const emptyModel: Model = {
  route: 'home',
  chapter: '',
  navHidden: false,
  drawerOpen: false,
  runtime: 'bun',
  searchOpen: false,
  caret: 0,
  searching: false,
  activeSection: '',
  player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
  version: '::[version]',
  query: '',
  focus: 0,
  copied: '',
  notes: seedNotes,
  seq: 3,
  notesOpen: false,
  barCollapsed: false,
  picking: false,
  noteTab: 'open',
  aimed: Option.none(),
  draft: '',
  highlight: Option.none(),
}

~

Whichever model the client starts from, the `routed` function takes the route from the address bar and sets it on that model. The address is what the reader asked for. A seeded model agrees with it only because the server keyed every rendered page by the same path, and in development no seeded model exists at all.

Leaving the route to the seed would boot every development address as the landing, since the default names the `home` route and nothing afterwards corrects it. An address the site does not serve names no route, and there the model's own route stands.

=>

const routed = (model: Model): Model =>
  Option.match(routeOf(window.location.pathname), {
    onNone: () => model,
    onSome: (route) => ({ ...model, route }),
  })

const flags: Effect.Effect<Model> = Effect.sync(() =>
  routed(
    Option.match(
      Option.fromNullishOr(document.getElementById('foldkit-model')?.textContent),
      {
        onSome: (text) => S.decodeUnknownSync(Model)(JSON.parse(text)),
        onNone: () => emptyModel,
      },
    ),
  ),
)

const application = Runtime.makeApplication({
  Model,
  Flags: Model,
  flags,
  init: (seed: Model): Step => [seed, []],
  update,
  view,
  subscriptions,
  container: document.getElementById('root'),
  hydrate: ssrHydration(),
})

Runtime.run(application)

